[NAME]
creature-server \- the server that runs April's Creature Workshop

[DESCRIPTION]
.B creature-server
is the brain of the show. It serves the REST and WebSocket API the Creature
Console and Creature World talk to, runs the 1 ms event loop that schedules
everything in real time, sends DMX/E1.31 to each creature, streams RTP/Opus
audio to the room, renders dialog through ElevenLabs, and keeps animations and
playlists in MongoDB.
.PP
It is normally started by systemd rather than by hand; see
.B SERVICE
below. The options are listed in
.B OPTIONS
and may also be supplied through the configuration file or the environment,
as described in
.BR CONFIGURATION .

[SERVICE]
Two units ship with the package, for the two deployments of the same binary.
Only the one you enable runs, so the other is inert.
.TP
.B creature-server.service
The mainstage installation.
.TP
.B creature-server-travel.service
The travel rig, which plays audio locally rather than over RTP.
.PP
Enable and start the one you want:
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl enable --now creature-server.service
.RE
.fi
.PP
Both units are installed to
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system
and are not configuration files. Because
.I /etc/systemd/system
takes precedence over that directory, local changes belong in a drop\-in rather
than in an edited copy of the unit:
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl edit creature-server.service
.RE
.fi

[CONFIGURATION]
Settings are resolved in three layers, each overriding the one before it:
.PP
.RS
configuration file < environment < command line
.RE
.PP
So a flag always wins, and the file is only the floor. The file is read before
the command line is parsed, because it supplies the defaults the parser falls
back on.
.PP
The configuration file is JSON. An
.B unknown key is a startup error
rather than something quietly ignored, which is deliberate: a typo in a key
name should stop the server, not silently change nothing. Keys beginning with
an underscore are treated as comments and ignored, so you can annotate the
file.
.PP
Secrets do not belong in the configuration file or in a unit file, both of
which are world\-readable. They belong in the
.B EnvironmentFile
described under
.BR FILES ,
which systemd reads as root before dropping privileges, so it can be mode 0600
and owned by root even though the service itself does not run as root.

[FILES]
.TP
.I /etc/creature/server.json
The main configuration file, shipped by the package and registered as a
conffile, so your edits survive an upgrade. Override the location with
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.I /etc/default/creature-server
Environment file read by both systemd units. This is where
.B VOICE_API_KEY
and
.B HONEYCOMB_API_KEY
go. Mode 0600, owned by root.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/creature-server.service
The mainstage service unit. See
.B SERVICE
above.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/creature-server-travel.service
The travel service unit.
.TP
.I /var/cache/creature-server
Regenerable cached data, owned by the
.B creature-server
system user the package creates. Safe to delete; it will be rebuilt.
.TP
.I sounds/
Sound files, relative to the working directory unless
.B \-\-sounds-location
says otherwise. The Opus packet cache lives in a hidden directory inside it
unless
.B \-\-audio-cache-dir
points elsewhere \(em worth doing when the sounds live on slow or backed\-up
network storage.

[EXAMPLES]
Ask the running binary what audio and network devices it can see, which is the
usual first step when configuring a new host:
.PP
.nf
.RS
creature-server \-\-list-sound-devices
creature-server \-\-list-network-devices
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run against a specific configuration file, for a second instance or a test:
.PP
.nf
.RS
creature-server \-\-config /etc/creature/server-test.json
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run the travel configuration by hand, playing audio out of the local sound
card instead of over RTP:
.PP
.nf
.RS
creature-server \-\-travel-mode \-\-local-audio \-\-network-device eth0
.RE
.fi

[SEE ALSO]
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
The server's own API documentation is served at
.I /api/v1/openapi.json
once it is running.
